<h2>Common claims for Hill Country properties</h2>
<p>Construction around Dripping Springs differs from the I-35 subdivisions east of San Marcos, and that changes how claims are evaluated.</p>
<h3>Metal and specialty roofs</h3>
<p>Standing-seam and exposed-fastener metal roofs are common on homes, barns, and outbuildings. Hail may dent panels without causing leaks, and some policies limit coverage for “cosmetic” damage. Read your policy’s roof endorsements and document dents carefully. Our <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage claims</a> page goes deeper.</p>
<h3>Outbuildings and fencing</h3>
<p>Barns, workshops, detached garages, and long fence lines are usually covered under the other-structures limit, which can be much lower than the dwelling limit. A complete inventory of damaged structures helps prevent items from being missed.</p>
<h3>Trees and ice</h3>
<p>Wind and ice bring large limbs down onto roofs, decks, and vehicles. Policies often handle the damage to the structure differently from tree removal, which may have its own small limit.</p>
<h3>Water and runoff</h3>
<p>Hill Country runoff moves fast. Water that rises from outside is generally a flood exclusion under a homeowners policy, while water entering through storm-created openings may be covered. See <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Businesses</h3>
<p>Commercial properties may have business income coverage, separate deductibles, and different documentation needs. Our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page explains what to gather.</p>

<h2>Filing after a widespread hailstorm</h2>
<p>After a storm like May 9, 2024, insurers, roofers, and adjusters across the area get busy at the same time. That can mean longer waits for inspections and estimates. Texas law sets deadlines in the Prompt Payment of Claims Act (Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542): an insurer generally has 15 days after notice to acknowledge the claim and begin its investigation, and 15 business days after receiving the information it requested to accept or reject the claim, with extensions allowed in some situations, including 15 extra days after a weather-related catastrophe declared by the insurance commissioner. Keep a written log of every contact and request. If your claim stalls, our <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a> page explains what we can do to help. For legal questions about deadlines, talk to a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>Permits in Dripping Springs</h2>
<p>The City of Dripping Springs has its own permit office, and its requirements are separate from the City of San Marcos. Ask the city what is required before roof replacement or structural repair. Properties outside city limits in unincorporated Hays County do not need a county residential building permit, but septic, development, and floodplain rules still apply. Code-required upgrades are paid only to the extent your policy’s ordinance or law coverage allows; our <a href="/local-building-codes/">building codes guide</a> covers this.</p>

<h2>How San Marcos Public Adjuster helps in Dripping Springs</h2>
<p>We represent policyholders, not insurance companies. Our work on a Dripping Springs claim usually includes a full inspection of the home and other structures, a policy review, a detailed estimate, and organized communication with the insurer, including follow-up under the Texas prompt payment deadlines. If the insurer’s estimate seems low, we can help with <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplements</a> or, when there is a dispute over the amount of loss, the <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal process</a>. We cannot guarantee any result.</p>
